---
import SnapshotCrossfade from '../../components/demos/SnapshotCrossfade.tsx';
import Frame from '../../components/diagrams/Frame.astro';
import SnapshotDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/SnapshotDiagram.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const capture = `// Set the mix to a known-good "menu" state, then capture it.
engine.bus('music').level = 0.8;
engine.bus('sfx').level   = 0.3;
const menu = engine.captureSnapshot('menu');`;

const explicit = `// Build a snapshot from explicit state — no need to mutate the live engine.
const boss = engine.snapshot('boss', {
  buses: {
    music: { level: 1.0, muted: false },
    sfx:   { level: 0.8, muted: false },
  },
  parameters: { intensity: 1.0 },
});`;

const apply = `await menu.apply({ fade: 0.25 });        // crossfade everything in 250ms
await boss.apply({ fade: 0.8 });`;

const blend = `const calm   = engine.captureSnapshot('calm');
// ...switch the mix to its combat shape, then re-capture.
const combat = engine.captureSnapshot('combat');

// Snap the mix to lerp(calm, combat, t). Drive a Parameter to animate.
engine.blendSnapshots(calm, combat, 0.4);   // 40% of the way to combat

const tension = engine.parameter('tension', 0);
tension.subscribe((t) => engine.blendSnapshots(calm, combat, t));
tension.set(0.75);                          // per-frame friendly`;
---
<Docs
  title="Snapshot"
  description="Capture a mix state. Apply it later with a smooth crossfade. Your menu/gameplay/boss switcher in one call."
  eyebrow="Concept"
>
  <p>
    Getting a mix right takes an afternoon of nudging eight sliders. Getting
    back to it from the menu screen takes eight <code>fadeTo</code> calls
    that all have to start together and land together, and one of them will
    drift the day someone adds a ninth bus.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <a href="/api/Snapshot/"><code>Snapshot</code></a> is an immutable
    record of bus levels, mutes and parameter values. You capture one when
    the mix sounds right and apply it later with a fade, in seconds, and the
    whole mix crossfades in one call.
  </p>

  <h2>Mental model</h2>
  <Frame caption="Three named snapshots. apply() ramps every bus level (and parameter) in lockstep.">
    <SnapshotDiagram />
  </Frame>

  <h2>API surface</h2>
  <Snippet code={`engine.captureSnapshot(name): Snapshot
engine.snapshot(name, state): Snapshot
engine.blendSnapshots(a, b, t): void   // snap mix to lerp(a, b, t)

class Snapshot {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly state: SnapshotState;
  apply(options?: { fade?: number }): Promise<void>;
  blendWith(other: Snapshot, t: number): void;
}`} lang="ts" title="Snapshot" />

  <h2>Live demo</h2>
  <p>Three presets, 800 ms crossfade between mixes.</p>
  <SnapshotCrossfade client:visible />

  <h2>Recipes</h2>

  <h3>Capture from the live engine</h3>
  <Snippet code={capture} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Build from explicit state</h3>
  <Snippet code={explicit} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Switch with crossfade</h3>
  <Snippet code={apply} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Blend continuously between two snapshots</h3>
  <p>
    <code>apply</code> is a one-shot crossfade. When you want a mix that
    follows a knob instead of a switch, capture two snapshots and let a
    <a href="/concepts/parameter/">Parameter</a> drive
    <code>blendSnapshots</code>. Game tension, distance to a boss room and
    time of day all read naturally that way.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={blend} lang="ts" />
  <p>
    Each call snaps the mix instantly, so calling it every frame is cheap.
    Every bus-level setter still rides its own 10 ms anti-click ramp
    underneath. Buses and parameters missing from either snapshot are
    skipped, and mute flips at <code>t = 0.5</code> rather than
    interpolating. The <a href="/examples/snapshot-blend/">snapshot-blend
    example</a> wires a slider through a Parameter end to end.
  </p>

  <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Snapshots don't include voices.</div>
    They restore the <em>mix</em>, not what is playing on it. For a "boss
    music starts" moment, trigger the boss music yourself, then apply the
    snapshot.
  </div>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Capturing twice does not snapshot motion.</div>
    A snapshot is a single moment. To animate a fade-in over time, drive a
    <a href="/concepts/parameter/">Parameter</a> instead.
  </div>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/parameter/">Parameter</a> handles continuous values.</li>
    <li><a href="/concepts/mixer/">Mixer</a> holds the bus state being captured.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
